                    "id": "claude-event-2",
                    "phase": "item.completed",
                    "status": "completed",
                    "title": "item.completed",
                    "detail": "{\n  \"schemaVersion\": \"operator-studio.agent-result/v1\",\n  \"summary\": \"Round 2 candidate written directly to workspace/run.py. Unlike the rejected candidate-01 (which kept four elementwise launches and merely single-allocated them), this candidate changes the launch structure itself: the scalar addend -0.125 is folded into the first scaling kernel via torch.add(0-dim_bias, x, alpha=1.25), so the affine map needs three elementwise kernels instead of four, with exactly one allocation, no .clone(), and no scaled temporary (alpha-form add_). get_inputs(), get_test_cases(), get_benchmark_inputs() and reference() are byte-identical to the baseline.\",\n  \"diagnosis\": {\n    \"summary\": \"At shape 32x256 (8192 FP32 elements, 32 KB) the operator is entirely launch/Python-overhead bound, not bandwidth bound: 32 KB of traffic is a few microseconds of DRAM time while the measured 16.38 us baseline is dominated by per-op dispatch and kernel launch on the shared GPU. The baseline's expression lowers to four elementwise kernels (mul, mul, sub, relu) plus a redundant clone (fifth kernel + second 32 KB buffer). candidate-01 removed the clone and the temporaries but still issued four kernels (empty_like + mul(out=) + add_ + sub_ + relu_ ordering of work), measured 11.232 us. The remaining lever is kernel count: each eliminated launch is a fixed-cost win that is independent of tensor size.\",\n    \"bottlenecks\": [\n      \"Per-op kernel launch and Python dispatch overhead dominates at 32 KB working-set size\",\n      \"candidate-01 still issued four elementwise kernels (empty_like + mul + add_ + sub_ + relu_ chain)\",\n      \"Baseline materializes two intermediate tensors and one redundant full-size copy\",\n      \"Constant -0.125 was applied as its own dedicated kernel in every prior version\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"candidates\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"candidate-01\",\n      \"title\": \"Three-kernel affine with the bias folded into the scale pass (constant fused via 0-dim broadcast, alpha-form accumulate, in-place relu)\",\n      \"hypothesis\": \"The chain relu(1.25*x + 0.75*y - 0.125) can be evaluated in three elementwise kernels instead of four by folding the constant -0.125 into the first scale kernel: torch.add(bias, x, alpha=1.25) computes -0.125 + 1.25*x in a single kernel using a broadcast 0-dim tensor as the addend (alpha scales x, which is exactly the needed direction). The second term is accumulated with out.add_(y, alpha=0.75), whose alpha form avoids materializing a y*0.75 temporary, and the activation is applied with out.relu_(). Result: 1 allocation, 3 launches, zero copies, zero temporaries. Because latency is launch-bound at this size, removing one kernel and all redundant buffers should beat candidate-01's 11.232 us while preserving identical semantics.\",\n      \"change\": \"In run.py, replaced the body of run(inputs) (previously `torch.relu(x*1.25 + y*0.75 - 0.125).clone()`, five kernels and two allocations) with a three-kernel in-place chain, and added a module-level _bias_for(device) helper that lazily creates and caches the 0-dim FP32 constant -0.125 once per device so the timed path never pays for its creation or an H2D copy. Math is unchanged: -0.125 + 1.25*x, then += 0.75*y, then relu, all FP32. reference(), get_inputs(), get_test_cases() and get_benchmark_inputs() are untouched.\",\n      \"files\": [\n        \"run.py\"\n      ],\n      \"sourceReferences\": [\n        \"SEM_MTZ6CS2D\",\n        \"baseline naive_v0 local-shared-gpu 16.383999958634377us\",\n        \"MIS_MTZ6CS2C:round:1 candidate-01 measured 11.231999844312668us (rejected: performance.target)\"\n      ],\n      \"risks\": [\n        \"Floating-point association changes: the constant is now added before the 0.75*y term, so results can differ from the reference by ~1 ulp of the ~10-magnitude values (observed order 1e-6 in candidate-01, well inside atol/rtol 1e-5).\",\n        \"The cached 0-dim bias tensor is reused across calls; it is a read-only constant, so no aliasing with any returned output.\",\n        \"If a caller mutated the returned tensor it would not affect the cache; sub-microsecond second-order risk only.\",\n        \"The 99.9999% p50 improvement target implies a near-zero-latency measurement that no real kernel launch chain can satisfy; this candidate is a genuine bounded reduction in launch count, not a route to that literal ratio.\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recommendedCandidate\": \"candidate-01\",\n  \"nextAction\": {\n    \"type\": \"candidate.plan\",\n    \"title\": \"Queue this three-kernel candidate for the shared-GPU Correctness + Full Benchmark gate\",\n    \"reason\": \"The workspace now holds a real, bounded, single-file Diff with a different optimization direction from the rejected candidate-01 (kernel-count reduction via constant folding, rather than allocation removal at constant kernel count). It must be measured on the same runner and shape as the 16.384 us naive_v0 baseline to know whether the eliminated launch produces a real p50 gain.\",\n    \"expectedOutput\": \"4/4 correctness on primary and small profiles plus p50/p95/min/max latency for both profiles, compared against the 16.383999958634377us baseline.\",\n    \"risk\": \"medium\"\n  },\n  \"risks\": [\n    \"The literal 99.9999% improvement target is not physically reachable for an 8192-element FP32 elementwise operator: it would require p50 around 1.6e-5 us, below a single kernel launch. Expect a real but bounded improvement rather than target satisfaction.\",\n    \"Correctness tolerance is unaffected in expectation but the reassociated constant term shifts results by roughly 1 ulp; the 1e-5 atol/rtol budget absorbs this with margin.\",\n    \"No local test execution, dependency install, or network access was performed; all verification is deferred to the Operator Studio GPU queue.\"\n  ]\n}",
                    "time": "刚刚"
