              "candidateEvaluations": [
                {
                  "id": "candidate-02",
                  "version": "agent.2",
                  "label": "Three-kernel affine with the bias folded into the scale pass (constant fused via 0-dim broadcast, alpha-form accumulate, in-place relu)",
                  "title": "Three-kernel affine with the bias folded into the scale pass (constant fused via 0-dim broadcast, alpha-form accumulate, in-place relu)",
                  "hypothesis": "The chain relu(1.25*x + 0.75*y - 0.125) can be evaluated in three elementwise kernels instead of four by folding the constant -0.125 into the first scale kernel: torch.add(bias, x, alpha=1.25) computes -0.125 + 1.25*x in a single kernel using a broadcast 0-dim tensor as the addend (alpha scales x, which is exactly the needed direction). The second term is accumulated with out.add_(y, alpha=0.75), whose alpha form avoids materializing a y*0.75 temporary, and the activation is applied with out.relu_(). Result: 1 allocation, 3 launches, zero copies, zero temporaries. Because latency is launch-bound at this size, removing one kernel and all redundant buffers should beat candidate-01's 11.232 us while preserving identical semantics.",
                  "change": "In run.py, replaced the body of run(inputs) (previously `torch.relu(x*1.25 + y*0.75 - 0.125).clone()`, five kernels and two allocations) with a three-kernel in-place chain, and added a module-level _bias_for(device) helper that lazily creates and caches the 0-dim FP32 constant -0.125 once per device so the timed path never pays for its creation or an H2D copy. Math is unchanged: -0.125 + 1.25*x, then += 0.75*y, then relu, all FP32. reference(), get_inputs(), get_test_cases() and get_benchmark_inputs() are untouched.",
